Lab 18: Monitoring and Logging Camel Routes
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Configure comprehensive logging for Apache Camel routes using SLF4J and Logback
Implement JMX-based monitoring for Camel routes and endpoints
Write a standalone Java tool that queries Camel JMX MBeans for route metrics
Analyze log files and JMX metrics to identify performance bottlenecks
Troubleshoot common Camel route issues using monitoring data
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Apache Camel concepts (routes, endpoints, processors)
Familiarity with Java development and Maven build tool
Knowledge of XML and Java configuration
Understanding of logging frameworks (SLF4J, Logback)
Basic knowledge of JMX (Java Management Extensions)
Experience with command-line operations in Linux
Your practice environment should have the following installed. If it does not, install them with:

# Verify Java (OpenJDK 11 or later)
java -version

# Verify Maven (3.8+)
mvn -version

# If missing, on a Debian/Ubuntu-based system you can install with:
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y openjdk-11-jdk maven
This lab is self-contained and uses only local processes on localhost. No external network access or special privileges are required, making it safe to run in an isolated practice environment.

