Expected Outcomes
A working Maven project producing rolling, level-filtered log files (camel-application.log, route-performance.log, camel-errors.log) driven entirely by Logback configuration
A running Camel application exposing live JMX metrics over an RMI connector on port 1099
A custom standalone JMX client (JMXMonitoringTool) that connects remotely and reports per-route state, completed/failed exchange counts, and mean processing time
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: JMXMonitoringTool reports a connection error such as "Connection refused" or "non-JRMP server at remote endpoint". Diagnostic hint: Confirm JMXMonitoringApplication is still running (jps -l should list it) and that ss -ltn | grep 1099 shows a listener before starting the tool; the RMI connector needs several seconds after startup to bind.

Issue 2: No files appear in data/output or route metrics show zero completed exchanges. Diagnostic hint: Check that the data/input directory exists and that the timer-based generator routes (test-data-generator or performance-test-route) have fired at least once by searching the console output or logs/camel-application.log for their route IDs; the file component only picks up files after they are fully written and the configured delay has elapsed.

Conclusion
In this lab, you configured a layered Logback setup that separated general application logs, route performance metrics, and error-only logs for an Apache Camel application, then verified each log stream by driving traffic through file-processing, timer, and error-handling routes. You then enabled Camel's JMX management with a remote RMI connector and built a standalone JMXMonitoringTool that connects over JMX to query live CamelContext and route MBeans, reporting state, completed and failed exchange counts, and mean processing time. Together these two tasks demonstrated the two complementary pillars of production Camel observability: structured, file-based logging for historical analysis and troubleshooting, and JMX-based live metrics for real-time health checks and alerting. As a next step, consider extending the JMX tool to poll on an interval and export metrics to a time-series format, or wiring Logback's ERROR_FILE appender to trigger an external notification when new entries appear.
